00:33:44.000 --> 00:34:44.000
A police spokesperson said, The killer can't have washed off all the blood, someone must have seen him, a friend or a relative probably, or maybe someone in the street, just so long as that someone tells us. This man is sick, very sick, and unless we catch him, he will strike again. Then came a promising discovery. Shortly after the double homicide, a police officer was searching a car in relation to a separate matter. In the boot of the vehicle, he found a leather sheath that held a blood-stained knife. It was 26 centimetres long, with a small curve at the tip of the blade that could have been caused by hitting something solid, like bone. The vehicle's owner strongly denied having anything to do with the Yeezy Street murders.
警方发言人说：“凶手不可能洗掉所有的血迹，一定有人见过他，可能是朋友或亲戚，或者可能是街上的某个人，只要那个人告诉我们。这个人病了，病得很重，除非我们抓住他，否则他会再次行凶。”随后出现了一个有希望的发现。双重谋杀案发生后不久，一名警察在处理另一起案件时搜查了一辆汽车。在车后备箱里，他发现了一个皮鞘，里面装着一把沾满血迹的刀。它长26厘米，刀尖有一个小弯曲，这可能是撞击坚硬物体（如骨头）造成的。车主强烈否认与Easy街谋杀案有任何关系。

36
00:34:44.000 --> 00:36:04.000
He insisted he had found the knife on the platform of the Victoria Park railway station, 450 metres northeast of the murder scene. He'd come across it on Monday, January 10, sometime between 10.20 and 11pm. Although this seemed highly suspicious, the owner of the vehicle was quickly ruled out. Not only did he have an alibi for the night of the murders, others also vouched for his story about finding the knife. Investigators ultimately discounted the knife as the murder weapon, given that it was found only 90 minutes after Sue Bartlett's brother, Martin, left Yeezy Street. It didn't seem possible that the killer could have conducted such a frenzied attack, spent time in the house cleaning himself up, and then made it to Victoria Park station in such a short time frame. Respected journalist Tess Lawrence covered the Armstrong and Bartlett case at the request of police. She attended 147 Yeezy Street when Suzanne and Sue's loved ones were permitted to enter the house to collect their belongings.
他坚持说他在维多利亚公园火车站的站台上发现了这把刀，该站位于谋杀现场东北450米处。他在1月10日星期一晚上10点20分到11点之间的某个时间发现了它。虽然这看起来非常可疑，但车主很快被排除了嫌疑。他不仅有谋杀案当晚的不在场证明，其他人也为他关于发现刀的故事作了担保。调查人员最终排除了这把刀作为凶器的可能性，因为它是苏·巴特利特的哥哥马丁离开Easy街仅90分钟后被发现的。凶手似乎不可能在这么短的时间内进行如此疯狂的袭击，在屋里花时间清理自己，然后到达维多利亚公园车站。受人尊敬的记者苔丝·劳伦斯应警方要求报道了阿姆斯特朗和巴特利特案。当苏珊和苏的亲人获准进入房子收拾遗物时，她去了Easy街147号。

37
00:36:04.000 --> 00:37:34.000
Published in the Herald newspaper, Tess's article detailed the layout of the house and the items within. Shortly after it ran, Tess received a phone call at work from a man whose voice she didn't recognize. He began dissecting the details of her article line by line and criticized Tess for not being very observant. Tess had mentioned there was a record player in the living room, the one Martin Bartlett had stopped by to fix on the Monday night, but she didn't mention the names of any of the records. The caller pointed this out. He then mentioned the name of one record, claiming it was on the turntable at the time. During the early days of the media coverage, it's believed that police intentionally fed journalists several incorrect details, so they'd be able to identify any misinformation or false confessions that might come through. Not only was it falsely reported that Sue Bartlett had also been raped, but a layout of the murder scene published in newspapers purposefully included a few minor discrepancies. When Tess Lawrence's article about the crime was published, she deliberately omitted the name of the record that was on the living room turntable at the time the murders were carried out.
苔丝的文章发表在《先驱报》上，详细描述了房子的布局和里面的物品。文章发表后不久，苔丝在工作中接到了一个男人的电话，她听不出他的声音。他开始逐行剖析她的文章细节，并批评苔丝观察不够仔细。苔丝提到客厅里有一台唱片机，就是马丁·巴特利特周一晚上过来修理的那台，但她没有提到任何唱片的名称。打电话的人指出了这一点。然后他提到了其中一张唱片的名称，声称当时唱片就在转盘上。在媒体报道的早期，据信警方故意向记者提供了一些不正确的信息，以便他们能够识别可能出现的任何错误信息或虚假供词。不仅有虚假报道称苏·巴特利特也遭到了强奸，而且报纸上公布的谋杀现场布局也故意包含了一些细微的差异。当苔丝·劳伦斯关于这起犯罪的文章发表时，她故意省略了谋杀发生时客厅转盘上那张唱片的名称。

38
00:37:34.000 --> 00:38:34.000
Tess was left stunned when the mysterious caller correctly named this record. She scrawled a quick note to a colleague asking to try and have the call traced, while simultaneously trying to keep the caller on the line. He refused to identify himself, but claimed to work at the Victoria Barracks. The caller said he'd phoned back before abruptly ending the call. Tess immediately reported the incident to the police. This was just one of thousands of tip-offs received in the first two weeks of the Armstrong-Bartlett murder investigation. At the time, Victoria's Homicide Squad only had 16 detectives. In the first 21 days of January alone, 11 murders had been committed throughout the state for an average of one murder every second day.
当神秘的来电者准确说出这张唱片的名字时，苔丝惊呆了。她匆忙给同事写了一张便条，要求设法追踪这通电话，同时试图稳住对方。来电者拒绝透露身份，但声称自己在维多利亚军营工作。来电者说完便挂断了电话。苔丝立即向警方报告了此事。这只是阿姆斯特朗-巴特利特谋杀案调查头两周内收到的数千条线索之一。当时，维多利亚州凶杀案调查组只有16名侦探。仅在1月的前21天里，全州就发生了11起谋杀案，平均每两天就有一人遇害。

39
00:38:34.000 --> 00:39:37.000
It was the largest number of murder cases on record since 1955. Resources were stretched incredibly thin. While the Easy Street Murders was one of the most brutal sex crimes in Victoria's history, investigators had several other unsolved cases vying for their attention. The owner of a massage parlor was shot to death outside his home, while another man had been stabbed in the throat with a broken beer bottle. A husband and wife were shot during a home invasion. The woman survived, but the man wasn't so lucky. None of the other murders were thought to be linked to the deaths of Sue and Suzanne, but those critical of the investigation believed the spiking crime meant the Easy Street case wasn't getting the undivided attention it deserved. Surely a case of that nature, which had terrified young women all over the city, should be top priority.
这是自1955年以来记录在案的谋杀案数量最多的一次。警力资源极其匮乏。虽然“易街谋杀案”是维多利亚州历史上最残忍的性犯罪之一，但调查人员还有其他几起悬案需要处理。一家按摩院的老板在家门外被枪杀，另一名男子被碎啤酒瓶刺穿喉咙。一对夫妇在入室抢劫中被枪击，女子幸存，但男子没那么幸运。虽然这些谋杀案被认为与苏和苏珊的死无关，但批评调查的人认为，犯罪率的激增意味着“易街”案件没有得到应有的全力关注。毕竟，这种性质的案件让全城的年轻女性感到恐慌，理应被列为重中之重。

40
00:39:37.000 --> 00:41:01.000
What the press and public didn't know was that investigators had already set their sights on a promising person of interest. 21-year-old crime reporter John Grant worked at the same newspaper as Easy Street resident Alona. Although the two colleagues weren't particularly close, they had gone out together on the night of Monday, January 11. Alona felt sorry for John. He'd been having a tough time lately. There were some rumours circulating about him at work, and he had a difficult home life. They ended up back at 149 Easy Street having drinks and playing pool in Alona's lounge room until the early hours of the morning. By 2am, it was too late for Grant to make his way home, so he decided to sleep on the couch. The next morning, he got a lift to work with Alona. Two days later, Alona went next door, where she discovered the bloodied bodies of Suzanne Armstrong and Susan Bartlett. When John Grant was questioned by police, he maintained he hadn't heard anything next door on the night he slept at Easy Street.
媒体和公众不知道的是，调查人员已经盯上了一位有嫌疑的重点人物。21岁的犯罪记者约翰·格兰特与“易街”居民阿洛娜在同一家报社工作。虽然两位同事关系并不特别亲密，但他们在1月11日星期一晚上曾一起外出。阿洛娜为约翰感到难过，他最近过得很艰难。工作中有关于他的传言，他的家庭生活也很不如意。他们最后回到易街149号，在阿洛娜的客厅里喝酒打台球，一直待到凌晨。凌晨2点时，格兰特回家已经太晚了，于是他决定睡在沙发上。第二天早上，他搭阿洛娜的车去上班。两天后，阿洛娜去隔壁时，发现了苏珊·阿姆斯特朗和苏珊·巴特利特血迹斑斑的尸体。当约翰·格兰特接受警方询问时，他坚称自己在易街过夜的那晚没有听到隔壁有任何动静。

41
00:41:01.000 --> 00:42:38.000
While this wasn't suspicious in and of itself, there was something about John Grant's presence in the neighborhood that immediately raised a red flag. 18 months earlier, a 19-year-old American woman named Julianne Garcia-Salay went missing from the flat she shared with her sister in North Melbourne. On the night she disappeared, Julianne invited three men whom she had recently met over to her flat to discuss the possibility of opening a restaurant. The men claimed that Julianne left at around 10.30pm to make a phone call from a public pay phone and never returned. They eventually got sick of waiting and decided to leave. Police weren't convinced of their story. Not only were several items missing from the flat, including $125 cash and a carving knife, but a blood-soaked towel was also found and a pair of Julianne's underwear was on the floor. Furthermore, one of the men who had visited Julianne that night was a known career criminal named Joseph John Power, who had faced prior murder allegations. One of the other men was John Grant. Grant was the kind of crime reporter who mingled with both police sources and criminal contacts, whatever it took to get a good story.
虽然这本身并不令人怀疑，但约翰·格兰特出现在那个街区的事实确实引起了警方的警觉。18个月前，一名叫朱莉安·加西亚-萨莱的19岁美国女子从她与姐姐在北墨尔本合租的公寓中失踪。失踪当晚，朱莉安邀请了她刚认识的三名男子到公寓讨论开餐厅的可能性。这几名男子声称，朱莉安在晚上10点半左右离开去公用电话亭打电话，之后就再也没回来。他们最终等得不耐烦，决定离开。警方并不相信他们的说辞。公寓里不仅丢失了几件物品，包括125美元现金和一把切肉刀，而且还发现了一条浸透鲜血的毛巾，地板上还有一条朱莉安的内裤。此外，当晚拜访朱莉安的其中一名男子是臭名昭著的职业罪犯约瑟夫·约翰·鲍尔，他曾面临过谋杀指控。另一名男子就是约翰·格兰特。格兰特属于那种为了拿到好新闻，既与警方线人打交道，又与犯罪分子接触的犯罪记者。

42
00:42:38.000 --> 00:44:11.000
His gift of the gab and hard drinking ways meant he could work both sides, but this time his close connections with the criminal world had left him in hot water. Grant claimed he had left Julianne's house before the other two men. He remembered it specifically because he'd called his editor and asked for permission to take a taxi home that could be reimbursed as a work expense. Police didn't necessarily think that Grant was involved with Julianne's disappearance. They did, however, suspect that Joseph John Power had something to do with it. The problem was that Power had recently made allegations of misconduct against Victoria Police after they accused him of another murder and an inquiry into his complaint was currently underway. Because of this, police were wary of investigating him for Julianne's disappearance and they didn't pursue the matter any further. John Grant claimed it was an unfortunate coincidence that he was with Julianne on the night she disappeared and next door to Suzanne Armstrong and Sue Bartlett on the night they were killed. Police interrogated him for an entire day, but he staunchly maintained his innocence. Alona said she didn't hear Grant get up or leave the house after he went to bed.
他能言善辩且嗜酒如命，这意味着他能游走于黑白两道，但这一次，他与犯罪世界的密切联系让他陷入了困境。格兰特声称他在另外两名男子之前就离开了朱莉安的家。他记得很清楚，因为他曾打电话给编辑，请求报销打车回家的费用作为工作开支。警方并不一定认为格兰特与朱莉安的失踪有关。然而，他们确实怀疑约瑟夫·约翰·鲍尔与此事有关。问题在于，鲍尔最近在被警方指控犯下另一起谋杀案后，对维多利亚警方提出了不当行为指控，针对他投诉的调查正在进行中。因此，警方对调查他是否涉及朱莉安的失踪持谨慎态度，没有进一步追究此事。约翰·格兰特声称，他与朱莉安失踪当晚在一起，又与苏珊·阿姆斯特朗和苏·巴特利特遇害当晚住在隔壁，这纯属不幸的巧合。警方审讯了他整整一天，但他坚决维护自己的清白。阿洛娜说，她没有听到格兰特在睡觉后起床或离开房子的声音。

43
00:44:11.000 --> 00:45:35.000
Her flatmate Janet was reading in bed until 4am and didn't hear anything either. When Alona woke up the next morning, Grant had already showered and was wearing the same clothes as the night before. They were clean and dry with no visible stains. There was nothing unusual about his behaviour that day or in the days following. Although there was no evidence to suggest that John Grant was involved with the Easy Street murders in any way, that didn't stop the speculation. After all, what were the chances that one crime reporter could be in the wrong place at the wrong time on two separate occasions? Police also believed the Easy Street killer had knowledge of forensic procedures which Grant did given his line of work. Furthermore, both the Easy Street crime scene and Julianne Garcia-Selaise flat shared a similarity. The presence of a blood-soaked towel and a pair of women's underwear left on the floor. Given Grant's association with the Joseph John Power, some wondered whether it was possible that Power had followed Grant to Easy Street on January 11 for an unknown reason.
她的室友珍妮特在床上看书直到凌晨4点，也没有听到任何声音。第二天早上阿洛娜醒来时，格兰特已经洗过澡，穿着和前一天晚上一样的衣服。衣服干净干燥，没有明显的污渍。那天以及随后的几天里，他的行为没有任何异常。虽然没有证据表明约翰·格兰特以任何方式参与了易街谋杀案，但这并没有阻止人们的猜测。毕竟，一个犯罪记者在两个不同的场合出现在错误的时间和错误的地点，这种概率有多大？警方还认为，易街的凶手具备法医知识，而格兰特凭借他的职业背景确实具备这些知识。此外，易街犯罪现场和朱莉安·加西亚-萨莱的公寓有一个共同点：地板上都留有一条浸透鲜血的毛巾和一条女性内裤。考虑到格兰特与约瑟夫·约翰·鲍尔的关系，一些人怀疑鲍尔是否可能出于某种未知原因在1月11日跟踪格兰特到了易街。

44
00:45:35.000 --> 00:46:55.000
Maybe he'd caught a glimpse of Suzanne Armstrong in her front bedroom and acted on impulse. Power was known to be a violent, dangerous criminal and he was physically capable of overpowering two unsuspecting women. It was a bit of a far-fetched theory, but every possibility had to be considered now that the investigation wasn't shaping up to be as straightforward as initially anticipated. The major hindrance was that nobody had witnessed anything suspicious around 147 Easy Street on the night of the murders. Or had they? Gladys Coventry was a veteran of Easy Street, having lived in number 145 since 1963. According to the book Murder on Easy Street by Helen Thomas, the elderly Gladys made a habit of sitting in her kitchen late on summer nights to take advantage of the cool evening breeze. Tuesday, January 11, 1977, was no different. But as Gladys enjoyed the fresh air on that hot night, she peered outside and noticed something at number 147.
也许他瞥见了前卧室里的苏珊·阿姆斯特朗，便一时冲动行事。鲍尔是一个众所周知的暴力、危险的罪犯，他有能力制服两名毫无防备的女性。这个理论虽然有些牵强，但既然调查不像最初预期的那样简单，每一种可能性都必须考虑。主要的障碍是，在谋杀案发生的当晚，没有人看到易街147号附近有任何可疑情况。或者说，真的没有人看到吗？格拉迪斯·考文垂是易街的老住户，自1963年以来一直住在145号。据海伦·托马斯的《易街谋杀案》一书所述，年迈的格拉迪斯习惯在夏夜深夜坐在厨房里，享受凉爽的晚风。1977年1月11日星期二也不例外。但当格拉迪斯在那个炎热的夜晚享受新鲜空气时，她向外望去，注意到了147号的一些情况。

45
00:46:55.000 --> 00:48:15.000
Her home was on the opposite side of the laneway to Sue and Suzanne's and from her vantage point, Gladys could see directly into their kitchen. There was a man standing at the sink, washing his hands repeatedly. It also looked like he was scrubbing on a piece of clothing. Gladys had never seen the man before, or if she had, she didn't recognise him. She didn't think anything of it. After all, her neighbours often had visitors, and from the casual, unhurried way the man moved about the house, nothing raised any alarm. It was only when Gladys heard about the murders several days later that she was reminded of the sighting. She approached a homicide detective to report what she had seen, but he didn't seem interested. At the time, the homicide squad had a reputation for being arrogant, and it wasn't until the case started to go cold that they realised they might have been too quick to dismiss Gladys' sighting. Detectives approached the elderly woman again, but she was offended that they didn't take her report seriously the first time, and she no longer wanted to speak to them.
她的家在苏和苏珊家巷子的对面，从她的位置可以直接看到她们的厨房。有一个男人站在水槽边，反复洗手。看起来他还在用力搓洗一件衣服。格拉迪斯以前从未见过这个男人，或者即使见过，她也不认识。她没多想。毕竟，邻居们经常有访客，而且从那个男人在屋里随意、不慌不忙的举动来看，没有任何令人警觉的地方。直到几天后格拉迪斯听说谋杀案时，她才想起这次目击。她找到一名凶杀案侦探报告了她所看到的情况，但他似乎并不感兴趣。当时，凶杀案调查组以傲慢著称，直到案件开始陷入僵局，他们才意识到自己可能太快忽略了格拉迪斯的目击证词。侦探们再次找到这位老妇人，但她对他们第一次没有认真对待她的报告感到不满，不再愿意与他们交谈。

46
00:48:15.000 --> 00:49:32.000
Detectives were so interested in the sighting that they even sent a doctor to Gladys' house under the guise that he was performing a welfare check, in the hopes he could get her to talk. But there was no fooling Gladys, who quickly sensed that he was with the police. The detectives realised that they had missed their chance to get a description of the man Gladys claims to have seen in the kitchen of 147, but instead of persevering, they accepted it and moved on. Had they taken Gladys' Coventry's sighting seriously from the start, they may have been able to get a physical description of the killer and an identity kit could have been drawn. But she wasn't the only neighbour who was overlooked by the police. 21-year-old Peter Sellers lived with his family at 139 Easy Street. On the night of Monday, January 11, he had a friend named Ray stay over while the rest of his family were travelling interstate. According to the book Murder on Easy Street, at around 2.30am both Peter and Ray thought they heard a front door slamming.
侦探们对这次目击非常感兴趣，甚至派了一名医生以进行福利检查为幌子去格拉迪斯家，希望他能让她开口。但格拉迪斯并没有被骗，她很快感觉到他是警察派来的。侦探们意识到他们错过了获取格拉迪斯声称在147号厨房看到的那个男人的描述的机会，但他们没有坚持，而是接受了现实并继续前进。如果他们从一开始就认真对待格拉迪斯·考文垂的目击证词，他们或许能得到凶手的体貌特征并绘制出身份拼图。但她并不是唯一被警方忽视的邻居。21岁的彼得·塞勒斯和家人住在易街139号。1月11日星期一晚上，他的家人都在州外旅行，他有一个叫雷的朋友留宿。据《易街谋杀案》一书所述，凌晨2点半左右，彼得和雷都以为他们听到了前门关上的声音。

47
00:49:32.000 --> 00:50:27.000
Moments later, they heard what sounded like two car door slamming before the car quickly took off. When police doorknocked the area once the murders were discovered, Peter wasn't home. However, his mother was there and she told them that her son had information that might be of interest. Police took a note of his name, but never returned to ask Peter what he had seen. Peter just assumed they weren't interested, so he did an attempt to follow it up. The failure to interview key witnesses was just one problem in a long list of failures in the investigation. One of the overriding issues was that the case was overseen purely by the State Homicide Squad, and Collingwood's criminal investigation branch was never brought in to assist.
片刻之后，他们听到了像是两扇车门关上的声音，随后汽车迅速驶离。当谋杀案被发现后，警方对该地区进行了挨家挨户的走访，彼得当时不在家。然而，他的母亲在家，她告诉警察，她的儿子可能有他们感兴趣的信息。警方记下了他的名字，但从未回来询问彼得看到了什么。彼得只是认为他们不感兴趣，所以也没有尝试跟进。未能采访关键证人只是调查中一长串失败中的一个问题。一个主要的问题是，该案完全由州凶杀案调查组负责，科林伍德的刑事调查部门从未被邀请协助。

48
00:50:27.000 --> 00:51:15.000
Had a local police been involved, their specialised knowledge of the area might have led to more possible persons of interest. It didn't help that days had already passed before the crime scene was discovered. This gave the perpetrator plenty of time to dispose of evidence or flee Melbourne. Secondly, although the crime scene was secured as soon as officers descended on the property, things were done differently in the 70s. Police were free to move through the crime scene without wearing any protective clothing. One officer had even washed his hands in the bathroom sink. Failure to maintain the integrity of the crime scene meant crucial evidence might have been missed.
如果当地警方参与其中，他们对该地区的专业知识可能会带来更多可能的嫌疑人。在犯罪现场被发现之前，已经过去了几天，这对调查毫无帮助。这给了肇事者充足的时间来销毁证据或逃离墨尔本。其次，虽然警察一到达现场就封锁了犯罪现场，但在70年代，处理方式有所不同。警察可以随意穿过犯罪现场，而无需穿戴任何防护服。一名警官甚至在浴室的水槽里洗了手。未能保持犯罪现场的完整性意味着关键证据可能已被遗漏。

49
00:51:15.000 --> 00:52:05.000
Complicating matters further was the fact that several detectives seemed convinced they already had their killer within sights. Many believed that John Grant's connection was too coincidental, and it would be a matter of time before evidence linked him to the crime. Others had the same feelings about Barry Woodard, the man Suzanne was dating at the time of her death. This tunnel vision meant that other persons of interest were potentially overlooked. During the short amount of time that Sue Bartlett and Suzanne Armstrong were living on Easy Street, major construction was taking place in the area. Not only was nearby Hoddle Street being widened in conjunction with the New Freeway,
更复杂的是，几名侦探似乎确信他们已经锁定了凶手。许多人认为约翰·格兰特的联系过于巧合，证据迟早会将他与犯罪联系起来。其他人对巴里·伍达德也有同样的感觉，他是苏珊遇害时正在交往的男子。这种隧道视野意味着其他嫌疑人可能被忽视了。在苏·巴特利特和苏珊·阿姆斯特朗住在易街的短时间内，该地区正在进行大规模施工。不仅附近的霍德尔街正在与新高速公路一起拓宽，

50
00:52:05.000 --> 00:52:52.000
but a new community health centre was being built right behind the women's home. Construction workers and other tradies were in and out of the site constantly. Was it possible that one of them had glimpsed into the backyard of 147 Easy Street and caught sight of Sue or Suzanne lounging in their backyard? Perhaps they'd taken an unhealthy interest in one or both of the women and returned to the property after dark. Similarly, concreting work had only recently been done outside of number 147. Could one of these workers have peered in through the front window, taken note of Suzanne and returned to approach her at the property later?
而且就在她们家后面正在建造一个新的社区卫生中心。建筑工人和承包商不断进出工地。有没有可能其中一人瞥见了易街147号的后院，看到了苏或苏珊在后院休息？也许他们对其中一人或两人产生了不健康的兴趣，并在天黑后回到了该处。同样，147号外最近才完成了混凝土浇筑工作。会不会是其中一名工人透过前窗窥视，注意到了苏，并随后回到该处接近她？

51
00:52:52.000 --> 00:53:29.000
Many of these workers were employed short-term and moved around often, so with police chasing other avenues in the early days of the investigation, it would have been easy for any of them to fall through the cracks. On top of the lackluster investigation, the Armstrong and Bartlett families weren't happy with how they were treated by the authorities. They were constantly chasing updates themselves rather than being kept in the loop with how things were progressing. Suzanne's family was never even interviewed by police.
这些工人大多是短期受雇，经常变动，所以当警方在调查初期追查其他线索时，他们中的任何一个人都很容易成为漏网之鱼。除了调查不力之外，阿姆斯特朗和巴特利特家族对当局对待他们的方式也不满意。他们一直在自己追问最新进展，而不是被告知事情的进展情况。苏珊的家人甚至从未接受过警方的采访。

52
00:53:29.000 --> 00:54:11.000
They had to make their own appointment to go down to the station and provide information. By July, six months had passed and police were no closer to making an arrest. An inquest into the murders was held at the Melbourne Coroners Court, but no new evidence was presented. The coroner concluded that Suzanne Armstrong and Suzanne Bartlett had been brutally killed by an unknown person or persons and shared no light on who that person might be. Suzanne's sister Gail was given custody of young Gregory,
他们不得不自己预约去警察局提供信息。到了7月，六个月过去了，警方在逮捕嫌疑人方面毫无进展。墨尔本验尸官法庭对谋杀案进行了调查，但没有提出任何新证据。验尸官得出结论，苏珊·阿姆斯特朗和苏珊·巴特利特被一名或多名身份不明的人残忍杀害，但没有说明那个人可能是谁。苏珊的姐姐盖尔获得了小格雷戈里的监护权，

53
00:54:11.000 --> 00:54:47.000
and in December, her family applied for victims of crime compensation on Gregory's behalf. This was a standard payment of up to $5,000 issued by the Victorian Crimes Compensation Tribunal for dependents of victims who were injured or murdered in a crime. In another blow to the Armstrong family, the tribunal rejected the application on the basis that Gregory was legally considered an illegitimate child because Suzanne hadn't been married to his father.
12月，她的家人代表格雷戈里申请了犯罪受害者赔偿。这是维多利亚州犯罪赔偿法庭为在犯罪中受伤或被谋杀的受害者的家属发放的最高5000美元的标准赔偿金。对阿姆斯特朗家族的又一次打击是，法庭驳回了申请，理由是格雷戈里在法律上被视为非婚生子女，因为苏珊没有与他的父亲结婚。

54
00:54:47.000 --> 00:55:20.000
When words spread about the rejection, the people of Australia were outraged. They rallied behind Gregory with the Victorian Premier announcing he'd make sure Gregory received compensation even if he had to change the laws to do it. The following year, the law was updated and Gregory received $5,000 compensation paid into a trust that he could access on his 18th birthday. The one-year anniversary of the murders passed
当拒绝赔偿的消息传开后，澳大利亚人民感到愤怒。他们支持格雷戈里，维多利亚州州长宣布，即使必须修改法律，他也会确保格雷戈里获得赔偿。第二年，法律进行了更新，格雷戈里获得了5000美元的赔偿金，存入了一个信托基金，他可以在18岁生日时支取。谋杀案一周年纪念日过去了，

55
00:55:20.000 --> 00:55:50.000
with no breakthroughs in the case, despite a $50,000 reward for information being announced. The public and investigators alike found it difficult to believe that the perpetrator continued to go undetected. Surely there must have been something in their behaviour after the crime that tipped somebody off. Perhaps this person had been a heavy drinker who avoided alcohol abruptly following the crime.
案件仍无突破，尽管已宣布悬赏5万美元征集线索。公众和调查人员都难以相信肇事者至今仍未被发现。肯定有什么行为在犯罪后引起了别人的注意。也许这个人曾经是个酒鬼，但在犯罪后突然戒酒了。

56
00:55:50.000 --> 00:56:25.000
Maybe their mental health had taken a sudden downhill turn. It was also hard to believe that someone would commit a crime so brutal and then never re-offend. Suzanne's sister Gail told the media, We'll never really get over this until the fellow is caught. You walk around not knowing who did the murder. It could be the bloke next door. One week later, the Armstrong family were again devastated
也许他们的心理健康状况突然恶化了。也很难相信有人会犯下如此残忍的罪行，然后就再也没有重犯。苏珊的姐姐盖尔告诉媒体：“除非抓住那个家伙，否则我们永远无法真正走出来。你走在街上，不知道是谁杀了人。那个人可能是隔壁的邻居。”一周后，阿姆斯特朗家族再次遭受重创，

57
00:56:25.000 --> 00:56:56.000
when Suzanne's father died from severe burns he had recently sustained from battling a bushfire. His was yet another life tragically lost within the Armstrong and Bartlett families in the aftermath of Suzanne's and Sue's murders. Sue's father had a fatal heart attack upon learning that his daughter had been killed. The next day, one of Sue's aunts also had a heart attack and passed away.
苏珊的父亲因在扑灭丛林大火时遭受严重烧伤而去世。在苏珊和苏遇害后，阿姆斯特朗和巴特利特家族又失去了一条宝贵的生命。苏的父亲在得知女儿遇害后心脏病发作身亡。第二天，苏的一位阿姨也因心脏病发作去世。

58
00:56:56.000 --> 00:57:23.000
Investigators kept an eye out for similar crimes that could potentially be linked but Easy Street remained in a league of its own. Some thought the killer might have already passed away perhaps by suicide or had been jailed for an unrelated offense. With no new information and other cases demanding attention the case went cold.
调查人员密切关注可能相关的类似犯罪，但易街案件依然是独一无二的。有些人认为凶手可能已经去世，也许是自杀，或者因无关的罪行被监禁。由于没有新信息，其他案件也需要关注，该案最终陷入了僵局。

59
00:57:23.000 --> 00:57:56.000
Cursed by its reputation as the murder house No. 147 Easy Street remained empty for six years as still and silent as the investigation itself. The house eventually sold at a discounted price and the new owner renovated it extensively tearing out the old wooden floorboards. Decades after the double homicide, the case was reopened. The new detectives casting their eyes on it for the first time
由于“谋杀屋”的名声，易街147号空置了六年，像调查本身一样沉寂。房子最终以折扣价售出，新主人进行了大规模翻修，拆除了旧木地板。双重谋杀案发生几十年后，案件重新开启。新侦探们第一次审视这些资料时，

60
00:57:56.000 --> 00:58:31.000
were expecting to have boxes of information to sift through. However, when they asked for the documents all they were given was a skinny manila folder containing a few flimsy statements. By the late 90s, forensic technology had improved dramatically and cold cases were starting to be solved using DNA. Victoria police were hopeful these advances would reveal the identity of the Easy Street killer once and for all. There were still eight key suspects on their list
本以为会有成箱的信息需要梳理。然而，当他们索要文件时，只得到一个薄薄的马尼拉文件夹，里面装着几份单薄的陈述。到了90年代末，法医技术有了显著提高，悬案开始利用DNA技术解决。维多利亚警方希望这些进展能一劳永逸地揭开易街凶手的身份。名单上仍有八名主要嫌疑人，

61
00:58:31.000 --> 00:59:04.000
including Barry Woodard and John Grant and each one of them agreed to provide a blood sample. DNA from their blood was tested against the semen of the killer. None were a match. This outcome was a major blow. For years investigators and the Armstrong and Bartlett police had held out hope that DNA would be the clue that finally held the answers. New leads had long since dried up
包括巴里·伍达德和约翰·格兰特，他们每人都同意提供血液样本。他们血液中的DNA与凶手的精液进行了比对。没有一个是匹配的。这一结果是一个重大打击。多年来，调查人员和阿姆斯特朗及巴特利特家族一直寄希望于DNA能成为最终揭开谜底的线索。新的线索早已枯竭，

62
00:59:04.000 --> 00:59:39.000
and now that the physical evidence eliminated the major persons of interest it seemed increasingly unlikely that the case would ever be solved. Then in September 1999 a postcard arrived at various media outlets across the city Postmarked from the town of Bright in northeast Victoria the writer of the postcards claimed to know who was responsible for the murders. The cards were forwarded to the Homicide Squad who wrote them off as a hoax.
现在物理证据排除了主要嫌疑人，案件似乎越来越不可能被解决了。然后，在1999年9月，全城的各个媒体机构收到了一张明信片。明信片上盖有维多利亚州东北部布莱特镇的邮戳，写信人声称知道谁应对谋杀案负责。这些卡片被转交给凶杀案调查组，他们将其视为恶作剧。

63
00:59:39.000 --> 01:00:13.000
Then in early 2004 a series of letters arrived at the home of Suzanne Armstrong who was the head of the police department and a series of letters arrived at the home of Suzanne Armstrong's mother and stepfather. Written in scrolling cursive they were from the same person who'd authored the postcards four and a half years earlier. His name was Peter Collier and he openly criticised the police for not taking his previous claims seriously.
随后在2004年初，一系列信件寄到了苏珊·阿姆斯特朗母亲和继父的家中。信件用潦草的连体字书写，来自四年前写明信片的同一个人。他的名字叫彼得·科利尔，他公开批评警方没有认真对待他之前的说法。

64
01:00:13.000 --> 01:00:51.000
As reported in the book Murder on Easy Street Peter had been a patient at Melbourne's La Rundle Psychiatric Hospital in early 1977. In late January his friend 30-year-old Anthony Thomas Christie was also admitted after attempting to end his own life. Christie spent 10 days in a coma before regaining consciousness. Three weeks later Peter was sitting in a group therapy session with seven other patients including Christie when Christie made a startling revelation.
正如《易街谋杀案》一书中所述，彼得在1977年初曾是墨尔本拉伦德精神病院的病人。1月下旬，他的朋友、30岁的安东尼·托马斯·克里斯蒂在试图自杀后也被收治。克里斯蒂在昏迷10天后恢复了意识。三周后，彼得正在与包括克里斯蒂在内的其他七名病人进行团体治疗，克里斯蒂做出了一个惊人的披露。

65
01:00:51.000 --> 01:01:21.000
After having sex with women he felt an overwhelming desire to kill them with a carving knife. Peter was stunned. He started to put two and two together. He knew Christie had a criminal past and the timing of the suicide attempt had him questioning whether Christie could have committed the Easy Street murders and then tried to take his own life because of it.
在与女性发生性关系后，他有一种强烈的欲望想用切肉刀杀死她们。彼得惊呆了。他开始把事情联系起来。他知道克里斯蒂有犯罪前科，自杀未遂的时间点让他怀疑克里斯蒂是否犯下了易街谋杀案，并因此试图自杀。

66
01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:46.000
In one of the letters to Suzanne's family Peter wrote This is only guesswork but I knew Christie and it's the only thing that makes sense to me. I don't believe for a moment that Suzanne was raped. It's possible but highly improbable. If you're capable of butchering two women you're capable of making out that one of them was raped.
在给苏珊家人的信中，彼得写道：“这只是猜测，但我认识克里斯蒂，这是唯一说得通的事情。我一点也不相信苏珊被强奸了。这有可能，但极不可能。如果你有能力屠杀两名女性，你就有能力伪造其中一人被强奸的假象。”

67
01:01:46.000 --> 01:02:11.000
A far more likely scenario is that Suzanne and Christie were involved in pillow talk. He's told Suzanne that he's killed a couple of crimps. He'd hinted that to me. The women have decided to get rid of Christie and they have threatened him with the police. He's lost control. They're dead. What would have made him lose control?
“一个更有可能的情况是，苏珊和克里斯蒂在枕边谈话。他告诉苏珊他杀过几个罪犯。他曾向我暗示过这一点。女人们决定摆脱克里斯蒂，并威胁要报警。他失去了控制。她们死了。是什么让他失去了控制？”

68
01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:45.000
He's hatred for the police. Peter explained that he didn't speak up at the time because he was suffering from poor mental health himself. He couldn't believe that the staff at La Rundle had failed to pass the information on to the relevant authorities. It haunted him to this day bolstered by the fact that Christie had since passed away without facing any consequences. In another letter, Peter wrote There is one way to get the truth out
“他对警察的仇恨。”彼得解释说，他当时没有说出来，是因为他自己的心理健康状况不佳。他无法相信拉伦德医院的工作人员竟然没有将这些信息传递给相关部门。这件事至今困扰着他，尤其是克里斯蒂后来在没有承担任何后果的情况下去世了。在另一封信中，彼得写道：“有一个方法可以查明真相，”

69
01:02:45.000 --> 01:03:20.000
and that is to put it out on the internet that Anthony Thomas Christie is the Easy Street Killer. That would put some pressure on the creeps covering up the truth. Suzanne's family reported the information to police who dismissed Peter Collier's theory. Apparently this wasn't the first time he'd made these kinds of claims to bereaved family members. It wouldn't be the last time either. In November 2005, Easy Street was a buzz
“那就是在互联网上发布安东尼·托马斯·克里斯蒂是易街凶手的消息。这会给那些掩盖真相的混蛋施加一些压力。”苏珊的家人向警方报告了这些信息，但警方驳回了彼得·科利尔的理论。显然，这已经不是他第一次向悲痛的家属提出这类主张了。这也不会是最后一次。2005年11月，易街引起了轰动，

70
01:03:20.000 --> 01:04:04.000
when several residents found a letter in their mailboxes from Peter. Written in black texta in all capitals, it read La Rundle Hospital protected Anthony Thomas Christie for the Easy Street murders for years. While it was clear that Peter had an ongoing fixation with the case there was no evidence to back his claims. Although if the killer was being held in a mental health facility that would explain why no similar crimes were committed after Suzanne Armstrong and Sue Bartlett's murders. That's one element that continues to baffle investigators.
当几位居民在信箱里发现了彼得的一封信时。信是用黑色记号笔全大写书写的，上面写着：“拉伦德医院多年来一直保护着易街谋杀案的凶手安东尼·托马斯·克里斯蒂。”虽然很明显彼得对该案有持续的执念，但没有证据支持他的说法。不过，如果凶手被关在精神卫生机构，那就能解释为什么在苏珊·阿姆斯特朗和苏·巴特利特遇害后没有发生类似的犯罪。这是令调查人员感到困惑的一个因素。

71
01:04:04.000 --> 01:04:40.000
It just didn't seem plausible that someone could commit such an extreme brutal murder then go on to live a normal life without ever killing again. By January 2017, four decades had passed and police were still no closer to making an arrest. If anyone other than the killer knew who was responsible they had either taken their secret to the grave or continued to live with the knowledge. In the hopes of enticing someone to talk
一个人犯下如此极端残忍的谋杀案，然后继续过正常生活，再也没有杀过人，这似乎不太可能。到2017年1月，四十年过去了，警方在逮捕嫌疑人方面仍无进展。如果除了凶手之外还有人知道是谁干的，他们要么把秘密带进了坟墓，要么带着这个秘密继续生活。为了诱使某人开口，

72
01:04:40.000 --> 01:05:20.000
Victoria Police announced a $1 million reward for information as well as possible indemnity against prosecution for anyone who came forward. A police spokesperson said we believe after 40 years someone out there knows something and it is time for them to come forward. These unsolved murders have been extremely devastating on these two young women's families and after all this time we would like to provide them with closure. For Suzanne Armstrong's family it was too little too late.
维多利亚警方宣布悬赏100万美元征集信息，并为提供线索的人提供可能的起诉豁免权。警方发言人表示：“我们相信40年后，有人知道一些事情，现在是他们站出来的时候了。这些悬案对这两个年轻女性的家庭造成了极大的破坏，经过这么长时间，我们希望能给他们一个交代。”对于苏珊·阿姆斯特朗的家人来说，这太迟了。

73
01:05:20.000 --> 01:05:51.000
Suzanne's sister Gail was still disappointed about the way the case was handled from the get-go but she was grateful that the investigation was still active after all these years. She told reporters This reward should have been offered 39 years ago and it would be solved and we wouldn't be doing all this now. I hear the miracles that police do solve cases and I think Suzanne will be next. They will do it.
苏珊的姐姐盖尔仍然对案件从一开始的处理方式感到失望，但她很感激这么多年后调查仍在进行。她告诉记者：“这个悬赏应该在39年前就提供，那样案件早就破了，我们现在也不用做这些了。我听说警方创造奇迹破获了案件，我想苏珊会是下一个。他们会做到的。”

74
01:05:51.000 --> 01:06:34.000
They will find this person. There were still 130 persons of interest on the list and a team was tasked with going through each individual in a bid to eliminate them once and for all. This was no easy task. Given how much time had passed 41 of those individuals had since passed away while others had relocated or changed addresses. Those who were able to be tracked down were encouraged to submit a voluntary DNA sample. The original eight suspects who had previously been cleared by DNA were also retested.
“他们会找到这个人的。”名单上仍有130名嫌疑人，一个团队被指派去逐一排查，试图一劳永逸地排除他们。这不是一项容易的任务。考虑到时间已经过去了这么久，其中41人已经去世，其他人则搬迁或更改了地址。那些能够被追踪到的人被鼓励自愿提交DNA样本。之前被DNA排除的最初八名嫌疑人也进行了重新测试。

75
01:06:34.000 --> 01:07:33.000
Once again there were no matches. Despite the age of the DNA evidence it still holds the key to solving the Easy Street murders. Investigators in Victoria haven't yet turned to genealogy testing to solve cold cases but hope remains that the perpetrator could finally be identified via an ancestral link. Veteran journalist Tess Lawrence who visited Easy Street as family members packed up Suzanne and Sue's belongings has never revealed the name of the record that was on the turntable in the living room at the time of the murders. She has this information safely stored away on the off chance it might ever be useful to the investigation. Collingwood today is not the suburb it was back in the 1970s.
再一次，没有匹配结果。尽管DNA证据年代久远，但它仍然是解决易街谋杀案的关键。维多利亚州的调查人员尚未转向基因检测来解决悬案，但仍希望通过祖先联系最终确定肇事者。资深记者苔丝·劳伦斯在家人收拾苏珊和苏的遗物时曾访问过易街，她从未透露过谋杀发生时客厅唱机上那张唱片的名字。她将此信息安全地保存起来，以备万一它对调查有用。今天的科林伍德已不再是1970年代的那个郊区了。

76
01:07:33.000 --> 01:08:49.000
